(adr (id "7d41e8c33f92") (version "1.0.0") (status 'approved) (for "d93037cecb98")
  (title "D14: Addresses name a MAILBOX, not a process; UUID required, alias local")
  (decision
    "An address is machine/user/entity, resolving to two routing-relevant parts:
     the NODE (machine+user -- what has a port and a spool) and the ENTITY (a
     named mailbox within that node's spool). The wire carries a UUID and the
     alias as a hint; the UUID decides. Aliases are LOCAL, ~/.ssh/config style,
     never announced into a shared namespace. Bindings form on first contact --
     TOFU is the chosen model and a CA is deferred, consistent with D12's
     no-PKI position.")
  (rationale
    "An entity is a MAILBOX, not a process, and the distinction is the whole
     point. `operator` is a human. `claude` may be several concurrent sessions.
     A long-running agent that restarts keeps its identity because the UUID
     persists in identity.sxp. Addressing a process id would mean every restart
     silently orphans mail, which is the failure a durable queue exists to
     prevent. Mail is therefore PEEKED, not consumed -- several readers may
     share one mailbox and a message that vanishes because something glanced at
     it is a message that got lost.

     A user is a security PRINCIPAL, not an instance: it owns the node, decides
     the spool location and sets the file permissions. That is why it sits in
     the node half of the address rather than the entity half.

     Instance-level targeting is available by giving an instance its own UUID,
     but it is usually the wrong choice for exactly the restart reason above.

     THE SAFETY PROPERTY that falls out: yxworker claims only <node>/yxworker.
     Anything addressed to another entity, or to a bare node (operator mail),
     is left untouched -- so adding an entity can never cause the automated
     dispatcher to swallow its messages, and nothing executes merely because a
     message arrived.

     UUID not GUID: same 128-bit value, but Microsoft's binary layout is
     mixed-endian where RFC 4122 is big-endian. Irrelevant while addresses are
     strings in s-expr; it matters the moment anything goes binary.")
  (source "laniakea/docs/contract-net.md#identity"))
